          "text": "# HIP 71352: A Bright Star With a Gaseous Veil\n\n*In Centaurus, this blue-white star offers an easy naked-eye find and a glimpse of the restless physics behind Be stars.*\n\nSome stars make their drama easy to see. HIP 71352 is bright enough to pick out without optical aid, yet its most intriguing feature is not an obvious change in the night sky. It is a B-type star whose catalogue classification, B2Ve, carries a clue: the \u201ce\u201d marks emission in its spectrum, a sign of gas around the star. HIP 71352 is also known as Eta Centauri, in the southern constellation Centaurus. ([SIMBAD](https://simbad.cds.unistra.fr/simbad/sim-id?Ident=HIP%2071352))\n\nThe \u201cBe\u201d in Be star describes a B-type star with emission lines in its spectrum. These stars are associated with fast rotation and surrounding gas; the gas can form a disk and contribute its own light at particular wavelengths. That activity is a matter for instruments and careful measurement, not something an observer should expect to see as a glowing ring through binoculars. The pleasure at the eyepiece\u2014or, here, the naked eye\u2014is subtler: knowing that a seemingly steady point of light has an active environment.\n\nHIP 71352\u2019s listed visual magnitude is 2.31, bright enough to stand out under ordinary dark skies. SIMBAD gives its J2000 coordinates as right ascension 14h 35m 30s and declination \u221242\u00b0 09\u2032. Its parallax, 10.67 milliarcseconds, corresponds by simple inversion to roughly 94 parsecs, or about 306 light-years. That distance is an estimate from the catalogue parallax, not a direct measurement of a tape-measure sort. ([SIMBAD](https://simbad.cds.unistra.fr/simbad/sim-id?Ident=HIP%2071352))\n\nFor a practical search, start with a current star chart or planetarium app and look for Eta Centauri in Centaurus; search by either \u201cEta Cen\u201d or \u201cHIP 71352.\u201d The star\u2019s southern declination makes it a rewarding target from southern latitudes, where it climbs high above the horizon. Farther north, it appears low in the southern sky or may not rise at all, depending on the observer\u2019s latitude. Give it a clear view toward the south and avoid choosing a night when haze or buildings swallow low stars.\n\nNo telescope is needed to find it. Under a reasonably dark sky, use the chart to identify the surrounding pattern, then confirm the star with unaided vision. Binoculars can help when you are learning the field, though they will not reveal the Be star\u2019s gas disk as a distinct shape. For a more deliberate observing session, note the date, time, location and sky conditions; compare the field with a chart, and return on another night. A star\u2019s apparent steadiness is part of the experience, but its classification reminds us that the spectrum can tell a more complicated story than the eye alone.\n\nThe almanac\u2019s small invitation is to look twice: first at the bright point in Centaurus, then at the science encoded in its name and spectrum. HIP 71352 does not need to flare or dramatically change to be worth observing. It is enough that a modest patch of southern sky holds a luminous B star, a surrounding-gas puzzle, and a reminder that familiar-looking starlight can carry evidence of motion and matter far beyond unaided sight.\n\n**Source:** [SIMBAD entry for HIP 71352 / Eta Centauri](https://simbad.cds.unistra.fr/simbad/sim-id?Ident=HIP%2071352)."
